422	 *     time, in the order they appear in the list (≈ pg_class order).
423	 *   - A concurrent `INSERT` into a child table grabs `RowExclusiveLock` on the
424	 *     child, then needs a `RowShareLock` on each FK-referenced PARENT to
425	 *     validate foreign keys (for example
426	 *     `agent_connections → organization/agents`).
427	 *   - If TRUNCATE has already locked the parent and the INSERT already holds the
428	 *     child, each waits on a lock the other holds → `40P01 deadlock detected`.
429	 *     Postgres aborts one victim; when the victim is OUR `db.begin(…)` TRUNCATE
430	 *     transaction, the error propagates out of `cleanupTestDatabase()` and fails
431	 *     whichever test's `beforeEach` ran it.
432	 *
433	 * The conflicting background statement completes in milliseconds, so retrying
434	 * the begin/TRUNCATE after a short backoff is deterministic recovery (the lock
435	 * the INSERT held is gone by the next attempt) — NOT a probabilistic
436	 * odds-lowering hack, and NOT a re-broadening of the 42P01-only catch. We retry
437	 * ONLY on 40P01, tolerate ONLY 42P01 (a listed table vanished mid-truncate),
438	 * and re-throw every other error and the final deadlock so a genuinely stuck
439	 * cleanup still surfaces.
440	 */
441	export async function truncateAllTables(
442	  db: postgres.Sql,
443	  quotedTables: string,
444	  canDisableTriggers: boolean
445	): Promise<void> {
