Here are the three SDK changes, against the real current surface (`packages/connector-sdk/src/connector-types.ts`).

## A. `relationships` on `eventKinds` — unblocks the whole spike branch

Today `eventKinds` carries exactly one declarative hook (`:543-556`):

```ts
eventKinds?: Record<string, {
  description?: string;
  metadataSchema?: Record<string, unknown>;
  attributions?: EventAttributionRule[];      // event → entity
}>;
```

`attributions` answers *"which entity does this event belong to"*. There is no way to say *"this invoice event implies invoice→customer"*. That's why every ERP edge is one imperative SDK call per edge. Proposed addition:

```ts
  relationships?: EventRelationshipRule[];    // entity → entity   (NEW)

export interface EventRelationshipRule {
  /** Relationship type slug. RESOLVED from the DB — never minted from this string. */
  type: string;
  /** Stable owner id within the manifest; connection + name = ownerId.
   *  Deliberately independent of ruleVersion so a bump keeps owning its edges. */
  name: string;
  from: RelationshipEndpointSpec;
  to: RelationshipEndpointSpec;
}
export interface RelationshipEndpointSpec {
  entityType?: string;
  identities: EntityIdentitySpec[];
}
```

Purely additive. The type-resolution constraint is non-negotiable and already implemented in the spike: a connector that could name a relationship type into existence could name an authz-bearing one.

## B. Identity scope — fixes #2798

`EntityIdentitySpec`'s own doc comment currently states the bug as if it were the design: *"UNIQUE on (organization_id, namespace, identifier)"*. That excludes `connection_id`, so two connections sharing a namespace silently collapse onto one entity — personal + work Gmail, two X accounts, two ERP tenants.

```ts
export interface EntityIdentitySpec {
  namespace: string;
  eventPath: string;
  matchOnly?: boolean;
  primary?: boolean;
  /** Uniqueness domain. Default 'organization' = today's behaviour. (NEW) */
  scope?: 'organization' | 'connection';
}
```

Defaulting to `organization` makes this non-breaking. This is *scope*, not cardinality — the distinction that made `uniquePerOrg` the wrong knob to reach for.

## C. `entityTypes` — #2804

There is no `entityTypes` field anywhere in `connector-types.ts`. A connector can *reference* a type but not *declare* one, so an org must run `config apply` before the connector produces anything typed. Proposed: a manifest declaration gated by an explicit install-time grant, so type creation stays reviewable.

Not SDK: valid-time (#2801) is a DB migration, and cardinality (#2803) is a relationship-type property.

Now the decisions I actually need.

